proof_plan:
For $m > n$:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = \map d {x_m, x_n}
| o = \le
| r = \sum_{j \mathop = n}^{m - 1} \map d {x_{j + 1}, x_j}
| c = [[Triangle Inequality]]
}}
{{eqn | o = \le
| r = \frac {k^n} {1 - k} \map d {x_1, x_0}
| c = [[Sum of Infinite Geometric Sequence]]
}}
{{end-eqn}}
So $\sequence {x_n}$ is a [[Definition:Cauchy Sequence (Metric Space)|Cauchy sequence]], and converges to some $x^* \in X$ by [[Definition:Complete Metric Space|completeness]].

$T$ is [[Definition:Continuous Mapping (Metric Spaces)|continuous]], so $T x^* = \lim T x_n = x^*$.
If $T y = y$ also, then $\map d {x^*, y} \le k \, \map d {x^*, y}$, so $\map d {x^*, y} = 0$.
{{qed}}
